Let $L$ be the length of the tr in meters.

We are given:
$v_j = 9$ km/h
$v_t = 45$ km/h
$d = 150$ m
$t = 25$ seconds

We need to find the length of the tr, $L$.

First, we convert the speeds from km/h to m/s:
$v_j = 9 \frac{\text{km}}{\text{h}} = 9 \frac{1000 \text{m}}{3600 \text{s}} = \frac{9000}{3600} \text{m/s} = \frac{90}{36} \text{m/s} = \frac{5}{2} \text{m/s} = 2.5 \text{m/s}$
$v_t = 45 \frac{\text{Km}}{\text{h}} = 45 \frac{1000 \text{m}}{3600 \text{s}} = \frac{45000}{3600} \text{m/s} = \frac{450}{36} \text{m/s} = \frac{25}{2} \text{m/s} = 12.5 \text{m/s}$

